-        "description": "Certify a Forge NormalizedVizSpec IR (the same IR viz.render emits as normalizedSpec), optionally with the chart's DATA. Returns a per-pillar verdict summary (pillars:{a11yEquivalence,determinism,contrast,accuracy} — five-valued on contrast, four-valued on accuracy) that disaggregates what was actually checked, plus — on the cartesian path — a folded conformance gate. Read-only: it reads the IR and the operand, never rebuilds or re-recommends.\n\nTWO COVERAGE CLASSES. The 5 cartesian types (bar/line/area/scatter/heatmap) return coverage:'certified' with a conformant boolean. conformant folds the graded pillars: 'fail' OR 'ungradeable' on contrast or accuracy pulls it false — ungradeable (s175) means grading was ATTEMPTED and failed for a reason outside the spec (an unresolvable canvas token, an evaluator fault); 'unchecked' (nothing to grade: no colour-bearing unit, no operand) and 'exempt' leave it a11y-driven. Both enums are five-/four-valued now (pass/fail/ungradeable/unchecked[/exempt]); an exhaustive consumer switch must handle 'ungradeable'. The 8 ECharts-primary types (treemap/sunburst/sankey/force_graph/choropleth/bubble_map/flow_map/chord) return coverage:'uncertified' / conformant:null — a DISTINCT verdict, not a failure, and NOT a statement that nothing was checked. Read the pillars, not the coverage value.\n\nTHE OPTIONAL `data` OPERAND (s172). An ECharts-primary IR is metadata-only by design (data:{values:[]}, encoding:{}) — the chart's nodes, links, rows and geometry live in viz.render's data branch and never enter the IR. Pass that SAME branch as `data` (exactly one of hierarchy | sankey | chord | network | geo, matching the mark trait) and certify lights two more pillars for these types AND runs the a11y-equivalence rules warn-first over it. Omit it and those two pillars stay 'unchecked' with a note naming the missing operand, and no a11y rule runs. `data` on a CARTESIAN spec is a structured error (OODS-V123): those certify from the IR alone, so it would be dead input.\n\nDETERMINISM. Cartesian: two independent Vega-Lite compiles, byte-compared, hashed — PLUS two independent @oods/viz-render renders byte-compared for every cartesian chart, including contrast-exempt charts (the first reported as the OPTIONAL determinism.renderHash; a nondeterministic render fails `stable` and pulls conformant false — the pillar is falsifiable, not a compile tautology). ECharts-primary WITH `data`: contentHash identifies the retained projected option from the first of two independent adapter emissions and still equals viz.render's for the same (spec, data) pair. renderHash identifies the first normalized SVG whenever server-side rendering succeeds; the second independently emitted option is rendered as the proof, and `stable` requires both option and render equality. A typed first-render fault keeps contentHash, omits renderHash, and makes stable false; bubble_map without inline geometry keeps the option proof and names the no-server-map limitation. The certified runtime contract and render-hash epoch live at packages/viz-render/certified-matrix.json. Scope, stated: certify claims nothing about what the CALLER rendered (the render leg is certify's own deterministic render); the render never feeds contentHash; and cross-process renderHash identity is bounded by the locally certified runtime axes in that record.\n\nACCURACY. Cartesian: four declared reader-only structural rules over the IR + the compiled spec — non-zero bar baseline (OODS-V150), dual axis (V151), area-encodes-linear (V152), aggregation-hiding (V153). ECharts-primary WITH `data`: a per-type set over the operand — treemap/sunburst V154 (a node value area or angle cannot encode) and V155 (an explicit parent that is not the sum of its children); sankey V156 (negative or non-finite link value), V157 (a node height that is not the flow its links carry) and V158 (a duplicate directed flow); chord V156; choropleth V159 (a region matched to rows with CONFLICTING joined values). force_graph, bubble_map and flow_map offer NOTHING — a stated position, not a gap, and s173 m01 splits it because the two halves differ: force_graph's distortion candidates are adapter constants, while bubble_map's colorScale and flow_map's strengthField ARE authorable through the branch and simply have no rule written for them yet (a scope decision on the record, not an impossibility). No scorer, no corpus, no render step; the rules read, so the contentHash is unmoved by them.\n\nREAD accuracy:'pass' PRECISELY: none of the rules OFFERED for that chart type positively detected its distortion — not that the chart is accurate. accuracySummary.rulesEvaluated says how many actually resolved their operand. On the ECharts path 'pass' additionally requires rulesEvaluated > 0, so zero resolved rules reports 'unchecked' with a note saying whether the offered set was empty or a precondition was absent. findings[] therefore carries THREE families told apart by code: OODS-A11Y-<rule.id>, OODS-V150..V153, OODS-V154..V159. On the CARTESIAN path a firing rule pulls conformant false; on the uncertified path conformant STAYS null (no folded claim is made there), so read pillars.accuracy and findings[].\n\nA11Y-EQUIVALENCE runs WARN-FIRST for these 8. Supply `data` and the 16-rule equivalence engine evaluates over the operand-built table and narrative, each rule reporting pass, fail, or NOT-APPLICABLE with its absent precondition named — so an absent precondition is no longer indistinguishable from a meaningful pass. Failures reach findings[] as OODS-A11Y-<rule.id> at their NATIVE severity (an error-severity rule reports 'error'; nothing is remapped and nothing blocks). Not-applicable rules reach the top-level a11yNotApplicable[] channel as {rule, preconditionAbsent} in rule order — present (as [] when no rule was not-applicable) exactly when the engine ran on this data-backed path, and ABSENT on a {spec}-only call, on the cartesian path, and when the engine faulted (the partial list is discarded and notes[] says so). A not-applicable rule never enters findings[]. pillars.a11yEquivalence stays 'unchecked' on this path DELIBERATELY: warn-first enumerates what enforcement will require without moving a verdict, and the flip to pass/fail is a future enforce step. Without the operand there is nothing to evaluate and no a11y findings appear. The accessible table and narrative are generated on the data-backed path.\n\nCONTRAST is RENDER-MEASURED for the 5 cartesian types: certify renders the compiled spec through @oods/viz-render and grades the SERIES-TO-PAINT ASSIGNMENT the data marks actually carry — duplicates retained, so a recycled palette (more consumed series than baked hexes) truthfully fails as a ΔE00=0 pair — against the requested CSS scope canvas: role-C mark-vs-background WCAG 3:1 (normative) + role-A categorical CIEDE2000 distinguishability (min-over-CVD), config.tokens overrides honoured. Unit classification still reads the compiled bytes, so a chart that baked no OODS palette can never certify contrast:'pass', and non-palette paints are author chrome — skipped, never failed. ECharts contrast is render-measured when `data` is supplied: visible chart fills and strokes feed Role C, the semantic N-long category assignment feeds Role A with genuine recycling duplicates retained, and the two grades combine by worst verdict. The 5 categorical families are graded; the 3 geo families remain exempt while still retaining normalized render evidence. spec-only calls retain the reconstructed baked-palette verdict and its baked-bytes caveat. An ordinal-categorical bubble_map colour is still not graded — as of s172 that rests on the exempt-all-geo ruling alone, since `data` exposes the colorField and the colorScale it renders on (s173 m01 corrects s172's wording: the RANGE is reachable on no path — the branch has no range field, so an ordinal bubble_map paints from Forge's own categorical list). contrastResults retains the requested theme/brand, verdict and measurement evidence; exempt and unchecked results do not claim a canvas ratio.\n\nTHEME AND BRAND (s190): theme light|dark and brand A|B select the same scoped CSS colors as viz.render; defaults are light/A. data-backed ECharts paints come from the rendered projected option; geo render evidence remains exempt with no canvas ratio. HC token exports exist, but HC pixels are deferred. accuracyRules names the offered rule codes; accuracySummary.rulesEvaluated still counts only resolved operands.\n\nIF YOUR CLIENT CACHES THIS TEXT (s173 m01): tool EXECUTION is read fresh from the built server on every call, so a Forge update reaches your results immediately — but this description and the input schema are what your connector advertised AT CONNECT TIME, so after a Forge release you are reading the old contract until you RECONNECT. When a Forge changelog says an input or a verdict moved, reconnect before trusting the schema you already have.",
+        "description": "Certify the Forge NormalizedVizSpec returned by viz.render. Returns coverage, a measured conformant result, findings, and four pillars: a11yEquivalence, determinism, contrast and accuracy. Read-only: it reads the IR and the matching data operand without rebuilding or recommending a chart.\n\nDECLARED COVERAGE. The five Cartesian types certify from the IR alone. The eight ECharts-primary types certify under the declared operand profile when the same data branch used by viz.render is supplied. ECharts conformance requires a11yEquivalence:'pass', contrast neither 'fail' nor 'ungradeable', stable determinism, and accuracy:'pass' with rulesEvaluated > 0 and no detected distortion. A certified chart can be conformant:false; inspect the pillars and findings. No Vega-Lite compile is added to the ECharts profile. Spec-only ECharts calls remain coverage:'uncertified' with conformant:null and notes naming the missing operand. Unmodeled marks remain explicitly uncertified.\n\nDATA. An ECharts-primary IR carries metadata; nodes, links, rows and geometry live in its original branch. Supply exactly the matching hierarchy, sankey, chord, network or geo branch as data. The tool validates it against the mark trait. Data on a Cartesian IR is OODS-V123 because those charts certify from their IR. HTML or invalid IR is OODS-V126.\n\nACCESSIBILITY. The 16 equivalence rules evaluate the operand-built table and narrative. Error-severity failures fail the ECharts pillar and conformance. Warnings stay visible without failing the pillar. Findings keep their native severity. a11yNotApplicable lists rules whose declared preconditions were absent; absence is not a meaningful pass. This channel is present when the ECharts engine completed, including [] if no rule was inapplicable, and absent for spec-only, Cartesian or incomplete engine evaluations. A completed engine with an individual rule fault retains its findings and not-applicable results while failing the pillar. An evaluator fault cannot produce a passing accessibility pillar.\n\nACCURACY. Cartesian rules read the IR and compiled spec: non-zero bar baseline (V150), dual axis (V151), area scaling (V152), undisclosed aggregation (V153). ECharts rules read data: invalid hierarchy values/non-additive parents (V154/V155); invalid sankey/chord link values (V156); inconsistent sankey node throughput/duplicate links (V157/V158); conflicting choropleth join values (V159); negative or non-finite bubble size (OODS-V168), radius-based bubble scaling (OODS-V169), conflicting rows at the same bubble coordinates (OODS-V170), negative or non-finite flow strength (OODS-V171), duplicate directed geographic flows (OODS-V172), and duplicate directed force-graph links (OODS-V173). Reciprocal graph links and reciprocal geographic flows are distinct. Bubble scaling reads the public renderer's default linear size behavior; certification does not repair its pixels. accuracyRules lists offered codes, while accuracySummary counts only rules whose preconditions resolved. Pass means no offered rule detected its distortion, not a general promise of accuracy; zero evaluated ECharts rules is unchecked and cannot yield conformant:true.\n\nDETERMINISM. Two independent compiler/adapter emissions are compared and rendered independently. contentHash identifies the first canonical compiled spec or projected ECharts option; renderHash identifies its normalized SVG and matches viz.render for the same operand/scope. Stable requires both identities to repeat. A first-render fault keeps contentHash, omits renderHash and fails stability. A bubble_map without inline geometry keeps the option proof, omits renderHash and names the no-server-map limitation. Proof is bounded by packages/viz-render/certified-matrix.json and covers certify's own rendering, not a caller's rendering.\n\nCONTRAST. Cartesian charts and ECharts calls with data grade actual rendered paints. Role C checks mark-to-canvas WCAG 3:1; Role A checks categorical CIEDE2000 distinguishability across normal vision and three CVD simulations, retaining recycled-palette duplicates. Missing baked OODS palette bytes cannot produce contrast:'pass'. The three geo families retain the standing contrast exemption with their render evidence; this includes ordinal bubble maps. Spec-only ECharts calls retain the reconstructed palette grade and caveat. Cartesian conformance still fails for fail or ungradeable contrast/accuracy; unchecked and exempt retain their existing semantics. Read every pillar's enum, including ungradeable. contrastResults reports the requested theme/brand and measurement evidence; exemptions do not claim a canvas ratio.\n\nSCOPE. Theme light|dark and brand A|B select the same CSS scope as rendering, default light/A. HC token exports exist, but HC pixels remain deferred. After a release that changes a schema or verdict, reconnect clients that cache tool descriptions and schemas.",
